2:T60e,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How much does a Bath Fitter-style bathroom renovation cost in the UK?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"As a rough guide, a limited bath or shower-area update may typically cost around £2,000 to £5,000, while a more complete bathroom renovation may typically cost around £6,000 to £12,000 or more. Every project and specification differs, so request a free, no-obligation quote for accurate pricing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is Bath Fitter cheaper than replacing a whole bathroom?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A surface-led update can cost less when the existing layout, plumbing and structure can be retained. If there are leaks, damp, damaged flooring or major layout changes, a full renovation may be more suitable."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long does a Bath Fitter-style installation take?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A straightforward surface-led installation may be quicker than a full strip-out, but the timescale depends on preparation, product availability, plumbing, electrical work and any repairs found during installation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can Bath Fitter-style panels be installed over existing tiles?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Some wall-panel systems may be suitable for installation over existing surfaces, but the wall should be stable, dry and properly prepared. Loose tiles, damp or uneven surfaces may need to be removed and repaired first."}}]}7:["slug","cost-bath-fitter-before-and-after","d"]
